Natas Level 5: Cookie Manipulation
What You See
Page displays: "Access disallowed. You are not logged in."
No buttons, forms, or anything to interact with
The Key Question
How does the website know I'm not logged in?
The Answer: Cookies
Every HTTP request you send includes cookies — small pieces of data stored by your browser and sent back to the server with each request.
Finding the Cookie
Open Developer Tools (Ctrl+Shift+I in Firefox/Chrome)
Go to Storage (Firefox) or Application (Chrome)
Click Cookies → natas5.natas.labs.overthewire.org
Find the cookie named loggedin with value 0
The Vulnerability
The server checks this cookie to decide if you're "logged in"
Value 0 = not logged in
Value 1 = logged in
This is client-side access control — the server trusts data the user can freely modify.
The Exploit
Change the cookie value from 0 to 1, then refresh the page.
The server sees loggedin=1 and serves the protected content.
Why This Is Insecure
Cookies are stored locally and easily modified
Authentication decisions should never rely solely on client-controlled data
Proper systems use server-side sessions with cryptographically secure tokens
